{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Draw near to the altar.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): to the north of the altar, for the slaughter of the sin-offering and the burnt-offering was in the north." "<b>And by</b> way of the Midrash: Aaron saw the altar in the shape of an ox and was afraid of it. Moses entered to him and said to him, 'Aaron my brother, do not fear that of which you are afraid; rather, lower your resolve,' and he approached it. Therefore it says, קרב אל המזבח." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, wrote: the intent of this Midrash is not that he saw upon the altar the shape of an ox, but the matter is that Aaron had no sin in his hand other than the sin of the calf, and that sin was fixed in his thought, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 51): ״וחטאתי נגדי תמיד״, and he saw the altar as though the form of a calf were there, holding back the offerings he was to offer from then onward; and therefore Moses said to him: you have no cause to fear this, for the sin has already been forgiven you." "<b>And make atonement for yourself and for the people, and offer the offering of the people.</b> It says first, 'and make atonement for yourself,' and afterward 'for the people,' that you should offer the offering of the people. <br><b>Or</b> one may interpret <b>'and for the people'</b> as referring to his family, that is, the house of Levi; afterward, <b>'the offering of the people'</b> — these are Israel."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {1 "Reading הגס דעתך as 'lower your resolve' (i.e., overcome your reluctance); the phrase is idiomatically difficult and rendered hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:17.990167387Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}